<a href="https://ollama.com">Ollama</a>, the popular app for running AI models locally on a computer, has released an update that takes advantage of Apple's own machine learning framework, <a href="https://opensource.apple.com/projects/mlx/">MLX</a>. The result is a hefty speed boost on Macs with Apple silicon.<br/>
<br/>
<img src="https://images.macrumors.com/article-new/2026/03/ollama-logo-mac.jpeg" alt="" width="1920" height="1080" class="aligncenter size-full wp-image-1043166" /><br/>
<a href="https://ollama.com/blog/mlx">According to Ollama</a>, the new version processes prompts around 1.6 times faster (prefill speed) and nearly doubles the speed at which it generates responses (decode speed). Macs with M5-series chips are said to see the largest improvements, thanks to Apple's new GPU Neural Accelerators.<br/>
<br/>
The update also includes smarter memory management, which should make AI-powered coding tools and chat assistants feel noticeably more responsive during extended use.<br/>
<br/>
Ollama says the new performance boost should especially benefit macOS users who run personal assistants like OpenClaw or coding agents like Claude Code, OpenCode, or Codex.<br/>
<br/>
